 # Multi-view projection
+Also called: Multi-view rasterization, Orthographic voxelization, 3-pass voxelization
+
 ## Overview
-Multi-view projection voxelization converts a triangle mesh into a voxel grid by rasterizing the geometry from multiple camera viewpoints, typically aligned with the principal axes of the scene's bounding box [G]. This approach solves the coverage gaps and geometric distortions inherent in single-view projection methods, which fail when surface normals are perpendicular to the view direction [G]. It differs from single-view methods by ensuring comprehensive coverage through redundancy, and from compute-shader-based methods by leveraging standard graphics pipeline rasterization [G].
+Multi-view projection generates a volumetric representation of a 3D mesh by rasterizing the geometry from multiple distinct viewpoints, typically aligned with the principal axes of the scene's bounding box. It solves the problem of occlusion and grazing-angle artifacts inherent in single-view projection methods by ensuring every surface element is visible from at least one viewpoint, thereby preventing "shadow" voxels [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Single 2D Projection Voxelization](3482-single-2d-projection-voxelization.md) by using multiple views to cover all surface normals, and from [GPU Voxelization via Compute Shaders](2549-gpu-voxelization-via-compute-shaders.md) by relying on standard rasterization pipelines rather than explicit ray-casting or compute-based intersection tests.
 
 ## Shared principles
-The family relies on the principle that projecting a 3D object from multiple orthogonal directions ensures that every surface element is visible from at least one viewpoint, preventing "shadow" voxels caused by occlusion or grazing angles [G].
+All methods in this family share the core mechanism of decomposing 3D occupancy into a set of 2D rasterization passes. The shared mathematical and procedural steps are:
 
-1. **View Direction Selection:** Cameras are positioned along the principal axes (X, Y, Z) of the scene's bounding box [G]. The view direction is parallel to the axis being projected onto [G].
-2. **Orthographic Projection:** Orthographic cameras are used to map 3D world coordinates directly to 2D screen coordinates without perspective distortion, simplifying the mapping from pixel coordinates to voxel grid indices [G].
-3. **Fragment-to-Voxel Mapping:** Rasterized fragments are transformed from clip space to voxel grid space [G]. The mapping uses the scene's bounding box and target resolution to determine the integer voxel coordinates $(i, j, k)$ [G].
-4. **Atomic Writes or Synthesis:** To handle concurrent writes from multiple triangles or views, atomic operations are used when writing directly to a 3D texture [G]. Alternatively, intermediate 2D buffers (sheets) are synthesized into a final 3D representation [G].
-5. **Degeneracy Handling:** Triangles parallel to a view direction may project to a line or point [G]. These cases are handled by either skipping the view for that triangle, expanding the triangle, or rasterizing along a different axis where the projection area is non-zero [G].
